<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Velvet Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Velvet Revolution was the peaceful overthrow of communist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in November and December 1989. Mass demonstrations began after police beat students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 17 November, and a general strike forced the Communist Party to give up its monopoly of power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civic-forum">Civic Forum</a> was elected president on 29 December, and free elections followed in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution.jpg" alt="Velvet Revolution" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ŠJů (cs:ŠJů) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Praha_1989-11-25,_Letn%C3%A1,_dav_(01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Communist Party seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> on 25 February 1948, and no official opposition parties operated afterwards. Dissidents, notably those of Charter 77, published home-made periodicals, and the secret police persecuted them. People were blacklisted for signing Charter 77, supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a>, promoting religion or having family in the West, and as all schools, media and businesses belonged to the state the rules were easy to enforce. By the late 1980s discontent with living standards had grown, and the changes in neighbouring countries, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a> on 9 November 1989, encouraged protest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 17 November 1989, International Students' Day, about 15,000 people joined a demonstration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, which marked the fiftieth anniversary of the killing of the student Jan Opletal by the Nazi authorities. Riot police stopped the marchers on Národní Street and attacked them. A false report that a student, Martin Šmíd, had been killed spread the next day and persuaded hesitant citizens to join the protests. Students and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> went on strike, and on 19 November Václav Havel and other dissidents founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civic-forum">Civic Forum</a> in Prague, with Public Against Violence formed the same day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>.</p><p>The number of protesters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> grew from 200,000 to an estimated 500,000 by 20 November. The entire Presidium of the Communist Party, including General Secretary Miloš Jakeš, resigned on 24 November, and on 27 November a two-hour general strike took place across the country, supported by a reported 75 per cent of the population. The Federal Assembly then removed the constitutional provision for the party's leading role. On 10 December President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a> swore in the first government in 41 years not dominated by the Communist Party, and resigned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> was elected president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> on 29 December 1989, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a> became speaker of the federal parliament the day before. Havel negotiated the removal of about 73,500 Soviet troops, who left within months. Free elections in June 1990 were the first democratic ones since 1946, and Czechoslovakia split peacefully into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> on 31 December 1992. The name Velvet Revolution was coined by Rita Klímová, the dissidents' English translator. Slovaks have called the events the Gentle Revolution. Some theorists, among them Jaroslav Krejčí, argue it was not a true revolution because it involved no illegitimate violence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Velvet Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marchers in Northern Ireland sang it before the shootings of 1972, hundreds of thousands sang it in Wenceslas Square during the <b>Velvet Revolution</b>, and Bengali versions were sung in the Bangladesh war of 1971.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>In Prague in 1989, during the <b>Velvet Revolution</b>, hundreds of thousands sang it in English and Czech in Wenceslas Square.</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a></li><li><blockquote>The split followed the <b>Velvet Revolution</b> of November 1989, which had ended communist rule, and the strengthening of Slovak national aspirations.</blockquote><a href="/state/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Velvet Revolution</b> of 1989 had ended communist rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/slovakia">Slovakia</a></li></ul></section>
